From 1d90622ac7d54a4b85309552d5fa1bf46adc3686 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.local>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 00:04:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] The desk could not name the producer of a single certificate,
 and the number it published was a guess

MEASURED ON THIS BOX, 2026-09-24. 174 ten-gate certificates in
UNIVERSAL_SURVIVORS.canon.json, provenance-shaped fields on none of them -- the
sealed gauntlet writes that file and knows nothing about producers. So against
~1,982 producers, 102 seats and 1.14M donation rows the desk could not say
which deserved compute, and the standing order to maximise conversion was
unmeasurable.

WORSE THAN THE GAP, THE PUBLISHED NUMBER WAS WRONG. ATTRIBUTION_COVERAGE.json
read `certificates: coverage 1.0, attributed 52/52` with a by_producer
histogram. Every one of those 52 came through attribution_census's
`symbol|family -> candidate` fallback, an index built with setdefault over
rowid order -- and between 105 and 437 candidates share each (symbol, family)
pair across six or more distinct producers. 52 arbitrary picks wearing a
measurement's clothes. Re-measured with exact keys only: 0 of 174 joined by
candidate id, donated_cell or grid_cell. All 174 took the fallback.

THE JOIN IS EXACT NOW, ON THE DESK'S OWN IDENTITY. certificate_provenance.py
recomputes frontier_identity.cell_id -- the rule the sealed gauntlet itself
names cells with -- from each store's own columns, over four independent
routes: the registry's birth stamp, the judge's docket, the gate ledger's
graph_id edge, and the hypothesis graph's own identity. MEASURED HERE:
174/174 named, 111 ATTRIBUTED (one claimant), 63 FIRST_CLAIM (co-discovery,
earliest by recorded time, every claimant listed), 0 UNMEASURED. A
symbol|family match is refused by construction, and a token that names a FILE
(external_backtest_results.json) is a route, not a producer.

THE JUDGED COLUMN LIED IN THE SAME ROW AS THE TRUTH. Read off
research_candidates.judged_at it gave `external` 1,246 judged cells and gave
every producer holding a certificate ZERO -- the same broken verdict join.
`judged` is now the gate verdict ledger joined by the SAME exact identity the
certificates use: 130,341 across the desk. `judged_stamped` is published
beside it under its own name so the contamination stays visible.

INCREMENTAL, SO IT RIDES A CLOCK IT DOES NOT OWN. A certificate is immutable
once minted, so a final record is never recomputed; only new cells pay for the
448 MB docket and the 4.2M-row graph. attribution_census calls it with its
REMAINING budget, which is how it gets hourly_cycle:attribution_census without
touching hourly_cycle.py.

Artifacts: data/certificate_provenance.json (the birth record, one row per
certificate with every claimant and route) and reports/PRODUCER_CONVERSION.json
(donations, docket, judged, certificates -- per producer, the table the desk
never had). 19 of 548 producers hold a certificate;
miner:discovery_compiler 68, breadth_sweep 21, miner:descendants:cross_market
17, alpha_registry 15, miner:descendants:instrument 11.

THE FENCE IS A SEVENTH AXIS, NOT AN EIGHTH CHECKER. check_birth_obligations
gains `certificate_birth`: a certificate the canon holds with no row in the
record is an arrival that fails. UNMEASURED WITH A REASON satisfies it; a
verdict with an empty `why` does not, because that is silence wearing a
verdict's clothes. tests/test_certificate_provenance.py drops the producer from
each link of the chain in turn and asserts the answer is UNMEASURED rather than
a name, and pins that a producer holding a certificate can never read 0 judged.

BARS UNCHANGED: gate_spec.yaml untouched, fixed_trial_count 109,
fixed_variance_of_sharpes 0.014863. Nothing retired, capped or shrunk -- an
unattributed certificate is a measurement gap, never a verdict.

